---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

const faqSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Is cedar or hemlock better for a sauna?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Neither species is automatically better. Western red cedar is lighter and its heartwood has stronger natural decay resistance. Western hemlock is denser and harder, with a paler and less aromatic appearance. Grade, moisture content, surface quality, assembly design, and the sauna manufacturer's instructions matter as much as species."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Can hemlock be used in an outdoor sauna?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "It can be part of a properly designed assembly, but the exterior shell, drainage, weather barrier, roof, ventilation, and maintenance plan must protect the structure. Do not assume interior cladding species alone makes an outdoor sauna weatherproof."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Should sauna wood be finished?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Follow the sauna or lumber supplier's instructions. Do not apply an ordinary paint, stain, or finish inside the hot room unless the product is explicitly approved for that sauna location and temperature. Exterior treatment is a separate weather-protection decision."
      }
    }
  ]
};
---
<Base
  title="Cedar vs Hemlock Sauna Wood: A Practical Comparison"
  description="Compare cedar and hemlock sauna wood by decay resistance, density, appearance, scent, maintenance, and the questions that matter before choosing a kit."
  canonical="/guides/cedar-vs-hemlock-sauna"
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />

  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
    <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-amber-700 mb-3">Material decision guide</p>
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-stone-900 mb-3">Cedar vs Hemlock Sauna Wood</h1>
    <p class="text-stone-500 mb-8">Reviewed August 2026 &middot; Backyard Sauna Pro</p>

    <div class="rounded-xl overflow-hidden mb-8">
      <img src="/images/lifestyle/sauna-cabins-outdoor.jpg" alt="Wood sauna cabins outdoors" class="w-full object-cover max-h-80" loading="lazy" width="1200" height="800" />
    </div>

    <p class="text-lg text-stone-700 mb-4">The useful answer is not simply "cedar outdoors, hemlock indoors." A sauna kit is a complete assembly: interior cladding, framing, insulation, weather protection, fasteners, ventilation, benches, and maintenance all affect durability.</p>
    <p class="text-stone-700 mb-8">Western red cedar gives you lower density, natural heartwood decay resistance, and a distinct aroma. Western hemlock is denser and harder, usually paler, and less aromatic. Choose after confirming the exact species, grade, moisture content, surface quality, and construction details.</p>

    <section class="bg-stone-900 text-white rounded-xl p-6 mb-8">
      <p class="text-amber-300 text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide mb-2">Short answer</p>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-3">Choose the better-built sauna, not just the preferred wood label</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-200 mb-5">For outdoor projects, prioritize the roof, base, drainage, weather barrier, and service access. For the hot room, prioritize dry, smooth, suitable-grade material and manufacturer-approved installation details.</p>
      <a href="/sauna-planner/" class="inline-block bg-amber-400 hover:bg-amber-500 text-stone-900 font-semibold px-5 py-3 rounded-lg" data-cta-position="above_fold">Build your sauna project brief</a>
    </section>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-10 mb-4">Evidence-based comparison</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
      <table class="text-sm w-full">
        <thead><tr class="text-left text-stone-500 border-b border-stone-200"><th class="pb-2 pr-6">Property</th><th class="pb-2 pr-6">Western red cedar</th><th class="pb-2">Western hemlock</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody class="text-stone-700">
          <tr class="border-b border-stone-100"><td class="py-3 pr-6 font-medium">Density and hardness</td><td class="py-3 pr-6">Lighter and softer</td><td class="py-3">Denser and harder in USDA tables</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-stone-100"><td class="py-3 pr-6 font-medium">Natural decay resistance</td><td class="py-3 pr-6">Heartwood is valued for natural resistance</td><td class="py-3">Do not rely on natural durability for weather exposure</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-stone-100"><td class="py-3 pr-6 font-medium">Dimensional movement</td><td class="py-3 pr-6">Known for dimensional stability</td><td class="py-3">USDA describes shrinkage as moderately high</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-stone-100"><td class="py-3 pr-6 font-medium">Appearance</td><td class="py-3 pr-6">Warm color with more visible variation</td><td class="py-3">Typically pale with a more uniform look</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="py-3 pr-6 font-medium">Scent</td><td class="py-3 pr-6">Distinctive and noticeable</td><td class="py-3">Usually milder</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 mb-8">Evidence source: the USDA Forest Products Laboratory's <a href="https://research.fs.usda.gov/fpl/wood-handbook" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-amber-700 hover:underline">Wood Handbook</a>, including its species, moisture, mechanical-property, and biodeterioration chapters. Natural properties vary by species, grade, heartwood versus sapwood, and individual board.</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-10 mb-4">When cedar is the stronger candidate</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-stone-700 mb-6">
      <li>You want a lighter wood with a warm, varied appearance.</li>
      <li>You like the characteristic aroma and have confirmed it is comfortable for your household.</li>
      <li>Natural heartwood decay resistance is useful within a properly weather-managed outdoor assembly.</li>
      <li>The supplier clearly identifies the species, grade, and construction details.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-10 mb-4">When hemlock is the stronger candidate</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-stone-700 mb-6">
      <li>You want a pale, comparatively uniform appearance.</li>
      <li>You prefer less wood aroma.</li>
      <li>You value a denser, harder surface and the kit protects the material from exterior moisture.</li>
      <li>The supplier documents kiln drying, board quality, and the full wall and weather assembly.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-10 mb-4">Five questions to ask the seller</h2>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-3 text-stone-700 mb-8">
      <li>What exact species and grade are used for walls, benches, framing, and exterior surfaces?</li>
      <li>Is the listed wood heartwood, sapwood, or a mix, and what moisture target is used before assembly?</li>
      <li>How are knots, splinters, fasteners, and high-contact bench surfaces handled?</li>
      <li>For an outdoor model, what protects the roof, walls, base, and penetrations from water?</li>
      <li>Which interior and exterior cleaning or treatment products does the manufacturer permit?</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="bg-emerald-50 border border-emerald-200 rounded-xl p-6 mb-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-stone-900 mb-2">Compare the total project, not a wood surcharge</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-700 mb-4">A better roof, base, ventilation plan, warranty, or installer may matter more than the species label. Put the full quotes side by side before deciding.</p>
      <a href="/tools/sauna-cost-calculator/" class="inline-block bg-emerald-800 hover:bg-emerald-900 text-white font-semibold px-5 py-3 rounded-lg" data-cta-position="cost_tool">Compare total project costs</a>
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-10 mb-4">FAQ</h2>
    <div class="space-y-5">
      <div><h3 class="font-bold text-stone-900 mb-1">Is cedar or hemlock better for a sauna?</h3><p class="text-stone-700">Neither is automatically better. Cedar favors natural decay resistance, low density, and aroma; hemlock favors a denser, paler, less aromatic surface. Construction quality remains decisive.</p></div>
      <div><h3 class="font-bold text-stone-900 mb-1">Can hemlock be used in an outdoor sauna?</h3><p class="text-stone-700">Yes, if the complete assembly protects it appropriately. Interior wood species alone does not make a sauna weatherproof.</p></div>
      <div><h3 class="font-bold text-stone-900 mb-1">Should sauna wood be finished?</h3><p class="text-stone-700">Follow the sauna and product manufacturers' instructions. Do not use an ordinary interior finish in the hot room unless it is explicitly approved for that location and temperature.</p></div>
    </div>

    <div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-xl p-6 mt-10">
      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-stone-900 mb-2">Related planning guides</h3>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm">
        <li><a href="/guides/sauna-wood/" class="text-amber-700 hover:underline">Sauna wood species guide</a></li>
        <li><a href="/guides/sauna-maintenance-guide/" class="text-amber-700 hover:underline">Sauna maintenance guide</a></li>
        <li><a href="/guides/outdoor-sauna-permits/" class="text-amber-700 hover:underline">Outdoor sauna permit planning</a></li>
        <li><a href="/guides/sauna-foundation-guide/" class="text-amber-700 hover:underline">Sauna foundation guide</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
